## Agent communication (token-conscious)

- **Avoid play-by-play**: Do not narrate every tool call or intermediate step ("First I will…", "Now reading…") unless the user asked for running commentary, the task is long or high-risk, or a short checkpoint is needed to coordinate (e.g. ambiguous requirements).
- **Batch work silently**: Prefer parallel tool use and uninterrupted execution; summarize once when there is a meaningful result.
- **Final message**: When work completes, give a **brief** outcome: what changed (files or areas), how to verify, and any follow-ups — not a single word like "done" unless the user explicitly asked for minimal replies for that thread.
- **Proportionality**: Match verbosity to task complexity; default to concise technical prose over tutorials.
- **New chat when scope shifts**: Unrelated work on a **long** thread still resends accumulated history each turn (more input tokens). Prefer a **new chat** for a clean scope; mention this only when it clearly helps (e.g. topic pivot), not every reply.
- **Prior context**: Do not read `agent-transcripts` or other session log folders unless the user explicitly asks for continuity from a prior run. Do not ask for full prior chat logs when a short description, error text, or `@` file reference is enough; avoid encouraging huge paste-ins unless the task truly depends on them.
- **Scoped reading (topics + source)**: Modular extensions and topic docs are there to **focus** work. Prefer the **relevant** topic files (from the index) and **source directly tied to the task** — files the user `@`-mentions, obvious edit targets, imports, messaging/event contracts — instead of opening large unrelated subtrees “just in case.” This is **efficiency guidance**, not a prohibition: if something is unclear, follow **one** dependency level, run a **narrow** search, or read an extra file; do not skip material that is **actually** needed for a correct or safe change.

