## Conventions

### Extension service structure
Most extensions keep service files alongside `extension.py` in the package root:
```
younite.<name>/younite/<name>/
├── extension.py            # IExt subclass, event wiring, lifecycle
├── some_service.py         # Feature service
└── another_helper.py
```
Larger extensions (e.g. `stage_core`, `navmesh_route`) organise into subfolders:
```
services/
├── kit_services/       # Feature-facing services
└── core_services/      # Infrastructure services (stage_core only)
```
All `from pxr import ...` statements must be **inside methods** (lazy imports), not at module level — avoids silent startup failures.

### Frontend UI placement

The frontend uses **React 18** with **react-bootstrap** for UI components and **Vite** for bundling. Do not install additional UI frameworks — use react-bootstrap and custom CSS.

The app has two modes via `AppModeContext`: **`cu`** (production Control Unit) and **`dev`** (developer tools). `StreamOnlyWindow.tsx` is the composition root — it nests the 6 context providers and conditionally renders view layers based on app mode.

| Folder | Purpose | View layer entry point | When rendered |
|---|---|---|---|
| `features/cu/` | Production user-facing UI (panels, overlays, menus) | `CuViewLayer.tsx` | `appMode === 'cu'` |
| `features/dev/` | Dev-only tools and controls | `DevViewLayer.tsx` | `appMode === 'dev'` |
| `features/streaming/widgets/` | Widgets shared between CU and dev | `SharedWidgetLayer.tsx` | Always |
| `features/streaming/overlays/` | Shared overlays (NPC bubbles, chat, video player) | `SharedOverlayLayer.tsx` | Always |

New UI components must be mounted through the appropriate view layer — don't render directly in `AppStream.tsx` or `StreamOnlyWindow.tsx`.

#### Reusable, modular UI

New work should be **modular and reusable** across CU, dev, and shared layers: extract shared controls (buttons, toolbars, pills, form patterns) into components that can be imported wherever needed — typically under `features/streaming/widgets/`, `features/streaming/overlays/`, or another obvious shared location — instead of reimplementing the same control separately in each view.

To avoid duplicating work **without** burning tokens on wide repo searches: use **`topic-index.mdc`** and the relevant **`.cursor/rules/topics/*.mdc`** files (plus this file) as the primary references; **ask the user** whether an existing shared component should be reused or extended when requirements are ambiguous. Do **not** run extensive multi-file or whole-codebase search passes just to “find a similar button” — rely on documentation and targeted `@` file references from the user when needed.

#### React context and state

The streaming feature owns shared React state via **`web-viewer-sample-main/src/features/streaming/contexts/`** (e.g. `StreamContext`, `NavigationContext`, `AppUIContext`, `ControlContext`, `EnvironmentContext`, `ChatContext`, etc. — see `contexts/index.ts`). When building or extending features, **prefer consuming or extending these providers** over scattering duplicate state in local `useState` at the leaves of the tree. **Add a new context** in that folder (and register it from the appropriate composition root, e.g. `StreamOnlyWindow.tsx`) when no existing provider fits. App-wide concerns may also use **`web-viewer-sample-main/src/context/`** — follow existing patterns there instead of introducing parallel ad-hoc state.

Local `useState` is still appropriate for **strictly local, ephemeral UI** (e.g. a single control’s transient open/closed flag). Anything that should **persist across sibling components, panels, or mode switches** should go through an existing or new context. This is complementary to **World State Sync** on the Kit side: world-affecting runtime truth still flows through the backend sync service; contexts orchestrate **frontend** coordination and UI state.

#### Styling vs logic

Keep **layout and visual styling out of component logic** for readability: use dedicated stylesheets (`.css`), CSS Modules, or clearly named co-located style files. **Avoid inline `style={{ ... }}`** except for values that are inherently dynamic (e.g. geometry from props, runtime measurements); even then, keep the inline surface minimal and prefer CSS variables or classes where possible.

#### Frontend icons (SVG / assets)

**Do not** scatter new `.svg` files under random feature folders. Add them under **`web-viewer-sample-main/src/icons/`** in an existing semantic subfolder (`map-markers/`, `chat/`, `navigation/`, `restrooms/`, `seat-route/`, `weather-season/`, etc.) or create a small new subfolder if none fits. Import with the Vite/TS alias **`@icons/...`** (see `vite.config.ts`) so paths stay short and agents can grep one tree.

- **Kit-driven filenames** (`interactions.json` `ui.icon` basenames, bird-eye markers) live only in **`icons/map-markers/`** — never rename without updating Kit config. Bulk loading: **`icons/kitMarkersRegistry.ts`** (and **`icons/iconBubbleAvatars.ts`** for `*_avatar` globs).
- **Reuse before inventing**: shared glyphs (e.g. media mute/volume) already live as React SVG components in **`features/cu/controls/MediaPlayerControls.tsx`** (`MediaVolumeOnIcon`, `MediaMuteIcon`, etc.).
- **Token efficiency**: one canonical asset root + `@icons` reduces blind repo-wide `.svg` hunts; prefer extending `src/icons/` over co-locating duplicates next to a single consumer.

